### Worked Example: My Own Move From WordPress to Next.js

My own site moved from WordPress to Next.js in February 2026. Google Search Console shows the search visibility before and after that move.

| Period | Platform | Clicks | Impressions |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 Jul 2025 to 19 Feb 2026 | WordPress | 18 | 12,998 |
| 20 Feb to 28 Oct 2026 | Next.js | 506 | 397,107 |

Source: Google Search Console, property sc-domain:sunnypatel.co.uk, checked 31 Oct 2026.

The rebuild also added new services pages and studies. The gain is platform plus content, not the platform alone. The two periods also differ in length. Read the table as a before and after record rather than a controlled test.

My WordPress to Astro move on a separate affiliate site cut duplicate H1s from 55 to 0. Schema coverage on that site rose from 34% to 100%. Source: my post-migration audit of that site, May 2026. The baseline tab is built to capture before and after counts like these.

### How to Use the Practice Tab

Mark each of the 14 redirects on the Practice tab as ok or error before you open the Answer Key. The redirects belong to a made-up shop. Eight of them hold a deliberate error. Each row has an Observed column with the recorded test result. Judge each row the way you would judge a crawl export.

1. Read each row and mark it ok or error in the last column.
2. Aim to find all 8 before you open the Answer Key tab.
3. Check yourself against the key. The key names each error and gives the fix.

The 8 errors are a chain, a loop, a redirect to the homepage, a 302, a trailing slash mismatch, a missing mapping, a mapping to an irrelevant page and a redirect to a 404. I look for the same faults in every real redirect map I review. Missing them on a live site costs far more than an hour of practice.

## The Real Cost of Getting This Wrong

Every old URL without a redirect returns a 404 on launch day. Google drops pages that keep returning 404 from its index. The rankings those pages held go with them. Links from other sites to those URLs stop sending visitors and value to your site.

One of my own sites went live without most of its redirects. shecookssheeats.co.uk is my Slimming World Syns lookup site. It moved from WordPress to Astro in late April 2026. On 9 May 775 old WordPress URLs were still in Google's index. Only 136 old URLs had a redirect. I added 635 more that day, after the new site was already live.

I cannot show what that gap cost. Clicks fell from about 22 a day (1 Mar to 23 Apr 2026) to about 6 a day (24 May to 23 Jul 2026). The steepest fall came after the redirects went in. It lines up with the May 2026 core update and with thin lookup pages on the same site that I later gated and rewrote. Clicks had also dropped 88% from October to December 2025 before any migration work. The redirect gap is a process failure I can document. The traffic loss has other likely causes too.

The lesson stands on its own. Put the redirects live on launch day.

Source: Google Search Console for shecookssheeats.co.uk, checked 1 Nov 2026.

The checklist above takes 2-3 days of preparation. Skipping it can cost months of traffic.

